### Performance and final reward: online agent rollout

After the correctness gate passes, the evaluator initiates **real coding-agent rollouts** against your service from an independent evaluation machine ({{ ar.agent }} is the agent framework, and **your deployed service itself is the agent's brain**). It runs {{ ar.n_attempts }} attempts on each of the following {{ d.rollout.n_tasks }} Terminal-Bench 2.1 tasks (concurrency {{ ar.n_concurrent }}):

  {{ d.rollout.subset_inline }}

Scoring is fully public:

- **Correctness gate: solved tasks ≥ {{ ar.min_solved }}** ({{ ar.min_solved_derivation }}). Fewer than {{ ar.min_solved }} → reward = 0.
- **Performance score: output throughput over the full rollout ÷ {{ vc.performance_baseline.output_throughput | round2 }} tok/s** (Oracle baseline under the same protocol).
- Per-task Oracle calibration values are also public in per_task_calibration in the evaluation configuration.

This workload measures correctness and performance together. If optimization harms model behavior (long-context attention, speculative-decoding acceptance, or sampling), the solved-task count drops first; if it only makes execution faster, the throughput score rises accordingly.

